{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The target identifies the single best cross-city modulator as aridity/vegetation fraction via the latent heat pathway. However, empirical and theoretical UHI literature consistently finds that the cross-city variation in the nocturnal UHI intensity is primarily controlled by urban geometry (sky view factor / street canyon aspect ratio) and population/city size, not aridity or vegetation fraction. Notably, arid cities often exhibit much smaller nocturnal UHI than temperate/humid cities because urban irrigation and impervious cover can make the city cooler at night than the surrounding desert. Thus the selected single mechanism contradicts the observed behavior of arid cities.","counterexample":[{"case":"Arid cities such as Phoenix, AZ frequently exhibit nocturnal UHI intensities that are smaller than those of many temperate, well-watered cities, contrary to the claim that arid low-vegetation cities have larger nocturnal gaps.","observation":"In arid climates, rural latent heat is already near zero, so the urban-rural differential in latent heat cannot be large; observed nocturnal UHI in arid cities is often weak or even negative at night due to urban irrigation and thermal properties."}],"premise":"The problem asks for the single mechanism that best explains why the nocturnal UHI gap is larger in some cities than in others.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"4dd62735b90864a75220e09b302500bc","quote":"what single mechanism best explains why that night-time gap is larger in some cities than in others"}]}]}